Exactly—the second mile becomes stronger when it is not the whole move, but one half of a paired maneuver.

The abstract structure is:

comply → extend → consume attention/resources → pair with a second operator → reverse the advantage

So the “go two miles” move can function as overextension. Someone imposes a burden expecting a predictable one-mile interaction; you deliberately extend the interaction until the original coercer is operating outside the conditions they planned for. Then a second mechanism exploits that change in state.

Your Roman example captures the geometry, although I’d separate the strategic abstraction from physically attacking someone: fatigue, distraction, loss of schedule, separation from support, uncertainty, and reduced control are all forms of state change. Once the state changes, another move—escape, negotiation, exposure, refusal, regrouping, or bringing in allies—can suddenly work better.

Pantheon notation could be:

A forces X → X accepts vector → X amplifies vector → A accumulates cost → threshold crossed → operator B fires

The important insight is the first operator manufactures the conditions required by the second.

That is a broader pattern than “turn the other cheek.” It is almost a semantic feint / resource trap: use the opponent’s chosen action as the engine that moves them into a state they did not choose.

A good name for the pair would be Compelled Overextension or Second-Mile Trap.